Cybersecurity Project Ideas (Boiled Down)
A curated list of practical, resume‑ready cybersecurity projects. Each includes difficulty, hireability, and what you actually learn.

1. Cloud Misconfiguration Lab
Difficulty: 5/10
Hireability: 7/10
Learning: 8/10
Summary:  
Simulate common cloud misconfigurations (IAM mistakes, storage exposure, network issues) and document how they happen and how to fix them.
Why it matters:  
Over 90% of cloud breaches come from misconfigurations. This is extremely relevant in 2026+.

2. Least Privilege IAM Design (Azure/AWS)
Difficulty: 5/10
Hireability: 7/10
Learning: 6/10
Summary:  
Build a permission model for a sample app. Document why each permission exists.
Why it matters:  
Identity is the new perimeter. IAM roles are understaffed and high‑paying.

3. GRC Gap Assessment (NIST or ISO)
Difficulty: 1/10
Hireability: 7/10
Learning: 5/10
Summary:  
Assess a fake company against NIST CSF or ISO 27001. Produce a gap report + risk register.
Why it matters:  
GRC roles are exploding in demand. This proves you can translate security into business language.

4. Active Directory Attack + Defend Lab
Difficulty: 7/10
Hireability: 8/10
Learning: 8/10
Summary:  
Build a small AD environment. Perform Kerberoasting, hash attacks, then detect and defend against them.
Why it matters:  
AD still runs most enterprises. Purple‑team skills are extremely hireable.

5. OAuth Consent Phishing Demo (Azure Entra ID)
Difficulty: 6/10
Hireability: 8/10
Learning: 9/10
Summary:  
Build a malicious OAuth app, simulate consent phishing, then detect and harden against it.
Why it matters:  
Identity attacks are the modern breach path. This is cutting‑edge cloud security.

6. Network Traffic Analysis (PCAP Hunting)
Difficulty: 5/10
Hireability: 6/10
Learning: 7/10
Summary:  
Load a malicious PCAP, hunt for C2 beaconing, document indicators.
Why it matters:  
Packet analysis is a core SOC skill. Shows you understand traffic beyond alerts.

7. Incident Response Playbook + Tabletop Exercise
Difficulty: 2/10
Hireability: 7/10
Learning: 6/10
Summary:  
Write an IR playbook, then run a tabletop exercise and document the results.
Why it matters:  
Leadership loves candidates who have practiced disaster recovery.

8. Build Your Own Vulnerability Scanner
Difficulty: 7/10
Hireability: 7/10
Learning: 9/10
Summary:  
Write a basic scanner: port discovery, service detection, CVE mapping.
Why it matters:  
Shows deep understanding of how scanning works under the hood — rare and impressive.

9. SOAR Automation Playbook
Difficulty: 6/10
Hireability: 8/10
Learning: 7/10
Summary:  
Automate alert enrichment, containment, ticket creation, or user lockdown.
Why it matters:  
SOC teams desperately need automation. This is future‑proof, AI‑aligned skill.

10. Find, Document, and Disclose a Real Vulnerability (CVE)
Difficulty: 4–100/10
Hireability: 10/10
Learning: 9/10
Summary:  
Find a vulnerability in a small app or device. Perform responsible disclosure.
Why it matters:  
A CVE with your name on it is one of the strongest solo credentials you can earn.